            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new word: what did tāpaḥ mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: भित्तिः",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "भित्तिः (bhittiḥ) — \"a wall\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "भित्तिः (bhittiḥ) is a wall: the built side of a room."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You know पुरम्, the town with a wall around it. भित्तिः (bhittiḥ) is one such wall brought down to the size of a house — the thing itself rather than the place it encloses."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It stands on bhid, \"to split, to break apart\", which is exactly what a wall does to a space: inside on one hand, outside on the other. Latin findere, \"to cleave\", is the same root, behind English fissure, and English bite belongs to it as well."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One wall, told apart from the walled town."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
