            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the last word of the chapter: what did sopānam mean, and what did stambhaḥ mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: तलम्",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "तलम् (talam) — \"the floor\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "तलम् (talam) is the floor: the flat under a room, and the underside of anything at all."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The palm of a hand is a tala, and so is the sole of a foot. The word names a flat bottom rather than a room in particular, which is why it reaches from a building to a body without changing shape."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin tellūs, \"the ground\", is a cousin of it. Neuter, ending in -अम्."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five parts of a building: a wall, a window, a stair, a pillar, the floor."
            }
          ]
        },
